The Nautilus DevOps team is setting up recurring tasks on different schedules. 
Currently, they're developing scripts to be executed periodically. 
To kickstart the process, they're creating cron jobs in the Kubernetes cluster with placeholder commands. Follow the instructions below:

Create a cronjob named xfusion.

Set Its schedule to something like */11 * * * *. You can set any schedule for now.

Name the container cron-xfusion.

Utilize the nginx image with latest tag (specify as nginx:latest).

Execute the dummy command echo Welcome to xfusioncorp!.

Ensure the restart policy is OnFailure.

Note: The kubectl utility on the jump-host has been configured to work with the Kubernetes cluster.

SOLUTION

CronJob Manifest
yaml
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: xfusion
spec:
  schedule: "*/11 * * * *"
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
          - name: cron-xfusion
            image: nginx:latest
            command:
            - /bin/sh
            - -c
            - echo Welcome to xfusioncorp!
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
Key Points Explained
apiVersion: batch/v1 – CronJobs live in the batch API group (stable since k8s 1.21).

schedule: "*/11 * * * *" – Runs every 11 minutes.

restartPolicy: OnFailure – Restarts the container only if it exits with a non-zero status (note: this goes at the pod spec level, not the container level).

command – We wrap the echo in /bin/sh -c so the shell interprets it (required since echo in a container needs a shell; alternatively you could use args but explicit command is clearer here).

Apply and Verify
1. Apply the manifest:

bash
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: xfusion
spec:
  schedule: "*/11 * * * *"
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
          - name: cron-xfusion
            image: nginx:latest
            command:
            - /bin/sh
            - -c
            - echo Welcome to xfusioncorp!
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
EOF
2. Verify the CronJob was created:

bash
kubectl get cronjob xfusion
Expected output:

text
NAME      SCHEDULE      SUSPEND   ACTIVE   LAST SCHEDULE   AGE
xfusion   */11 * * * *  False     0        <none>          5s
3. Inspect full details (confirm image, command, restartPolicy):

bash
kubectl describe cronjob xfusion
4. (Optional) Trigger a manual job to test the command immediately:

bash
kubectl create job --from=cronjob/xfusion xfusion-manual-test
kubectl logs job/xfusion-manual-test
You should see: Welcome to xfusioncorp!

5. Clean up the manual test job afterwards:

bash
kubectl delete job xfusion-manual-test
The CronJob will now fire automatically every 11 minutes and produce a Job pod that runs the placeholder command with the nginx:latest image.
